Make an answered COMMIT, rename or stable write mean the data is on the drive,
whatever the export says.

The macOS client writes UNSTABLE and then sends COMMIT, and takes the reply as
its data being durable. nfsd answers a COMMIT with vfs_fsync_range() over the
client's range, and on an async export with nothing. On ntfs3 a range fsync is
generic_file_fsync, which writes the file's pages and inode and not the
volume's own metadata, which only syncfs(2) writes. Measured on a real drive on
2026-09-10: after a 32 MiB write and fsync from the Mac, 464 kB was still dirty
in the guest; an fsync of the file cleared 208 kB of it, and syncfs of the
volume the other 256 kB.

COMMIT now syncs the file's filesystem as syncfs(2) does, with s_umount held
for reading, then flushes the device, so the metadata the sync wrote is behind
the flush, and reports a writeback error the filesystem met on the way.

An async export spares every create and remove a sync of its own (29 ms and
10 ms each on the test drive, against none), but nfsd also makes a stable write
unstable there while telling the client it is stable, and skips a rename's
metadata commit. A stable write is now written through on any export, and a
rename on an async export syncs the filesystem, once its locks are dropped,
before it is answered: renaming over a file is how applications save safely. A
create, remove or attribute change is still answered before it is on the drive,
as a journalling filesystem answers them before its journal is written, and is
on it by the next COMMIT or rename.

The read-only nfsd parameter commit_is_durable says a kernel has this, and
vmproxy exports async only where it finds it.

